const W=(id,name,tagline,icon,color,visual,lessons)=>({id,name,tagline,icon,color,visual,lessons:lessons.map((l,i)=>({...l,id:`${id}-${i+1}`,world:id,index:i}))});
const L=(title,concept,analogy,flow,example,trap,defense,question,options,correct,why,visual,link)=>({title,concept,analogy,flow,example,trap,defense,question,options,correct,why,visual,link});
window.CURRICULUM=[
W('fundamentals','Ground Zero','Learn to think like a security engineer','◎','#f4a76e','layers',[
L('What cybersecurity protects','Security reduces the probability and impact of unwanted outcomes; it is not a promise that nothing bad will happen.','Like protecting a museum: you safeguard exhibits, visitors, access, and the ability to reopen.', ['Identify assets','Identify likely threats','Choose safeguards','Measure residual risk'],'A hospital protects patient records (data), the scheduling service (availability), and correct prescriptions (integrity).','A checklist is not proof that the system is safe.','Start with a threat model and verify important controls.','Which is an asset?',['Only a server','A patient database and the service using it','Only passwords'],1,'Assets include information, systems, processes and services.'),
L('CIA triad','Confidentiality prevents unauthorized disclosure; integrity prevents unauthorized modification; availability keeps systems usable.','Sealed envelope, tamper-evident seal, and a dependable delivery service.',['Confidentiality: control readers','Integrity: detect tampering','Availability: withstand disruption','Balance all three'],'Encryption protects a backup from disclosure, but you still need integrity checks and restore tests.','Encryption alone does not guarantee availability.','Use access controls, integrity checks, redundancy and backups.','A website is unreachable during a traffic flood. Which property failed?',['Confidentiality','Integrity','Availability'],2,'The service is unavailable despite its data potentially remaining secret.'),
L('Threat, vulnerability, exploit, risk','A threat is a possible source of harm; a vulnerability is a weakness; an exploit is a way to use it; risk combines likelihood and impact.','A burglar (threat) notices an unlocked window (vulnerability) and climbs through (exploit).',['Asset: customer records','Threat: unauthorized access','Weakness: missing authorization','Risk: exposure of records'],'A public storage bucket is a weakness; whether sensitive records are in it affects impact.','A CVE severity score is not your organization’s complete risk score.','Prioritize by exposure, exploitability, business impact and existing controls.','A public cloud bucket with sensitive backups primarily illustrates what?',['A vulnerability with potential impact','A cryptographic hash','A firewall rule'],0,'The exposed configuration is a weakness and the contents increase impact.'),
L('Security principles','Least privilege, deny by default, segmentation and defense in depth reduce the blast radius when a control fails.','A building uses a reception desk, keycards, locked rooms and cameras—not a single door.',['Verify identity','Grant minimal access','Separate trust zones','Monitor exceptions'],'An application identity that only reads one database table cannot delete all backups if stolen.','Zero trust does not mean zero connectivity; it means no implicit trust.','Make permissions small, time-bound and reviewable.','Which permission is least-privilege for a report viewer?',['Database administrator','Read-only access to reporting data','Root on every host'],1,'Read-only access directly supports the required task.'),
L('Authentication vs authorization','Authentication answers who you are. Authorization answers what that identity may do. Both must be checked server-side.','Checking a ticket identifies a guest; scanning the ticket at the VIP door checks entitlement.',['Claim identity','Verify credentials','Create session','Authorize every protected action'],'A regular user logs in successfully but must still be blocked from /admin/invoices.','A valid login does not authorize all objects and API routes.','Enforce object- and function-level authorization on each request.','A user sees another user’s invoice after changing an ID. What failed?',['Authentication','Authorization','DNS'],1,'The server did not enforce ownership of the requested object.','access'),
L('Attack surface and trust boundaries','The attack surface includes externally and internally reachable entry points. A trust boundary is where data crosses different security assumptions.','A parcel passes from public sidewalk to lobby to secure archive; each crossing needs checks.',['List entry points','Trace data movement','Mark trust boundaries','Validate inputs and permissions'],'Browser input becomes server parameters and then a database query; trust changes at both transitions.','Internal requests and headers are not automatically trustworthy.','Map data flows and validate at every meaningful boundary.','Where is a trust boundary?',['Between untrusted browser input and server logic','Inside a string literal','Between two identical comments'],0,'Data changes trust assumptions when it crosses from browser to server.'),
L('Defense in depth','Multiple independent controls reduce the chance one failure becomes a breach; controls should detect and contain, not merely block.','Seatbelt, airbags, road design and emergency response all reduce harm in different ways.',['Prevent','Detect','Contain','Recover'],'MFA prevents many account takeovers; logs detect suspicious access; access limits prevent broad loss.','Duplicating two identical vulnerable controls is not strong defense in depth.','Choose complementary controls and test failures.','A strong password policy replaces the need for monitoring.',['True','False','Only in cloud'],1,'Monitoring helps detect threats that bypass preventive controls.'),
L('Ethics and safe testing','Authorization, defined scope, minimal impact and evidence handling are requirements of professional testing.','A fire drill happens by agreement and in a controlled environment.',['Get permission','Define scope','Use a controlled lab','Report findings responsibly'],'Practice injection against the deliberately simulated queries in this game, not unknown public websites.','A publicly accessible target is not automatically authorized for testing.','Use your own systems, CTFs and explicit written permission.','Which target is appropriate for your first injection practice?',['An unknown store','An explicitly authorized training lab','A friend’s production app without permission'],1,'A training lab provides authorization and an intended learning environment.')
]),
W('networking','Wire & Protocol','Follow packets from cable to application','⇄','#83baa9','packet',[
L('TCP/IP and OSI','OSI is a conceptual seven-layer model; the practical TCP/IP suite groups functions into fewer layers. Encapsulation adds headers as data moves down.','Letters go in an envelope, into a mailbag, then onto a truck.',['App: HTTP request','Transport: TCP segment','Internet: IP packet','Link: Ethernet frame'],'HTTP data can ride inside TCP, which rides inside IP, which rides inside an Ethernet frame.','Layer names help troubleshooting but real protocols sometimes span layers.','Locate failures layer-by-layer instead of guessing.','Which layer handles IP addressing?',['Internet / network','Application','Physical'],0,'IPv4 and IPv6 operate at the Internet / network layer.','layers'),
L('IPv4, CIDR and subnets','An IPv4 address identifies an interface; CIDR prefix length determines the network portion and address range.','Street address plus neighborhood boundary.',['Read /24 prefix','24 network bits','8 host bits','256 total addresses'],'192.168.10.0/24 spans 192.168.10.0 through 192.168.10.255; ordinary hosts use .1–.254.','Total addresses are not the same as usable host addresses on traditional IPv4 LANs.','Segment networks and apply ACLs based on actual routing requirements.','How many total IPv4 addresses are in a /28?',['16','28','256'],0,'32 − 28 = 4 host bits, so 2⁴ = 16.','layers'),
L('TCP three-way handshake','TCP uses SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK to establish initial sequence-number state for a connection.','Two people agree they can hear one another before exchanging information.',['Client → SYN','Server → SYN-ACK','Client → ACK','Application data begins'],'A SYN flood exhausts connection-related resources by sending many incomplete attempts.','TCP delivery is reliable and ordered, not encrypted by default.','Use SYN cookies and rate-limiting where appropriate; add TLS for confidentiality.','Which packet is second?',['ACK','SYN-ACK','FIN'],1,'The server acknowledges the SYN and proposes its own sequence number.','packet'),
L('UDP vs TCP','UDP sends datagrams without TCP’s connection establishment or built-in retransmission; applications can build their own reliability.','A postcard versus a tracked conversation.',['Choose latency needs','Choose ordering needs','Choose application protocol','Handle losses appropriately'],'DNS often uses UDP for ordinary queries; modern HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP with its own reliability.','UDP is not synonymous with insecure; security depends on the protocol built above it.','Understand protocol features before applying firewall rules.','HTTP/3 commonly runs on which transport?',['TCP only','UDP through QUIC','ICMP'],1,'QUIC is built over UDP and supplies secure transport features.'),
L('DNS resolution','A recursive resolver locates authoritative answers via root, TLD and authoritative DNS servers and caches records by TTL.','A librarian asks a city directory, then a street directory, then the building caretaker.',['Browser asks resolver','Resolver checks cache','Hierarchy finds authority','IP answer returned'],'A record maps a hostname to IPv4; AAAA maps to IPv6; MX helps route email.','DNS lookup does not prove the destination server is legitimate; TLS identity validation is separate.','Use trusted resolvers and DNSSEC validation where appropriate.','Which DNS record commonly maps a host to IPv4?',['MX','A','TXT'],1,'An A record holds an IPv4 address.','dns'),
L('HTTP, cookies and sessions','HTTP is stateless; sessions and cookies can associate multiple requests with a logged-in identity.','A coat-check ticket connects later requests with your stored item.',['GET /login','POST credentials','Server issues session','Browser sends cookie'],'A session cookie should normally be Secure, HttpOnly and use an appropriate SameSite setting.','Cookies are automatically sent in matching contexts; CSRF defenses still matter.','Rotate session IDs on login and enforce server-side expiration and authorization.','HttpOnly primarily helps prevent what?',['JavaScript reading the cookie','All CSRF','Every XSS bug'],0,'HttpOnly blocks document.cookie access but is not a complete XSS or CSRF fix.','auth'),
L('TLS and HTTPS','TLS authenticates the server (usually with certificates), negotiates keys, then protects application data in transit.','A trusted notary checks the recipient before both parties use a private conversation channel.',['ClientHello','Server certificate + handshake','Validate identity and derive keys','Encrypted application data'],'HTTPS secures the transport link, not the integrity of code on a compromised server.','A padlock does not mean the website owner is trustworthy.','Validate certificates and use current protocols and configuration.','Does HTTPS stop malicious JavaScript intentionally served by the website?',['Yes','No','Only for POST'],1,'TLS protects transmission, not the trustworthiness of endpoint content.','handshake'),
L('Firewalls, NAT and routing','Routers forward packets between networks; firewalls enforce policy; NAT translates addresses and is not, by itself, a security policy.','Road signs choose routes; checkpoints enforce entry rules.',['Packet arrives','Route chosen','Policy evaluated','Forward or deny'],'Allow web clients outbound TCP 443 while denying unsolicited inbound connections to desktops.','NAT is not a substitute for firewall and endpoint protections.','Write minimal allow rules and log important denies.','Which device function chooses the next hop?',['Routing','Hashing','Authorization'],0,'A router uses its routing table to pick the next hop.','firewall'),
L('VLANs and segmentation','VLANs separate Layer-2 broadcast domains; inter-VLAN routing plus ACLs control communication between them.','Rooms on the same floor can be separated by doors and permissioned corridors.',['Assign ports to VLANs','Trunk tagged traffic','Route where needed','Enforce ACLs'],'Put lab machines in VLAN 30 and production assets in VLAN 10 with explicit access rules.','Different VLAN IDs alone do not guarantee isolation once routing is enabled.','Document traffic paths and validate allowed/denied flows.','What enables communication across IP subnets?',['A router / L3 switch','A plain unmanaged hub','A DNS TXT record'],0,'Cross-subnet traffic requires routing.','firewall')
]),
W('systems','Host & Identity','Understand the machine, user and privilege boundaries','⌘','#b8a5d2','auth',[
L('Linux filesystem and permissions','Linux permissions describe read, write and execute privileges for user, group and others. Directories use execute to traverse.','Keys to read a document, edit it or enter its cabinet.',['Owner','Group','Others','r=4, w=2, x=1'],'chmod 640 report.txt gives owner read/write, group read, others none.','777 is not a troubleshooting strategy; it grants unnecessary write access.','Prefer precise ownership, groups and restrictive file modes.','What does 600 mean for a regular file?',['Everyone can read','Only owner can read and write','Everyone can execute'],1,'6 = read + write for owner; other digits are zero.','layers'),
L('Processes and privilege','Processes run with identities and permissions; administrator or root privileges increase potential impact.','A contractor should hold only the key required for one room.',['Start process','Assign identity','Use required privileges','Audit elevated actions'],'A web service should not run as root unless strictly necessary.','A compromised low-privilege account may still reach sensitive user data.','Separate service accounts, use privilege boundaries and patch escalation paths.','Why avoid root for everyday services?',['It increases blast radius','It makes DNS faster','It encrypts files'],0,'Unnecessary privileges amplify the damage of compromise.'),
L('Windows and event logs','Windows event channels record security and system activity; event context matters more than a single ID.','A flight recorder needs timestamps, actors and a sequence of events.',['Collect events','Normalize fields','Correlate timeline','Investigate context'],'Event 4625 records a failed logon; numerous failures may reflect mistyped passwords or spraying.','One failed authentication is not automatically an incident.','Correlate host, account, source and rate before escalating.','What helps distinguish mistype from password spray?',['Patterns across many accounts','A single timestamp','Screen brightness'],0,'Spraying often produces failures against multiple identities from related sources.','triage'),
L('Active Directory basics','Active Directory stores directory objects and centralizes identity; domain controllers authenticate and serve directory operations.','A campus directory and access office shared by many buildings.',['Users and groups','Domain controllers','Policies and trusts','Resources check access'],'A group can grant access to shared storage; Group Policy can configure host settings.','Being domain-joined is not the same as being a domain administrator.','Minimize privileged groups and monitor changes.','What does a domain controller primarily support?',['Central identity and domain services','Image compression','GPU rendering'],0,'AD domain controllers provide authentication and directory functions.','auth'),
L('Kerberos simplified','Kerberos uses a Key Distribution Center and tickets so services can verify identities without sending passwords to every service.','A trusted ticket office issues passes recognized by attractions.',['AS exchange → TGT','TGT requests service ticket','Client presents service ticket','Service validates ticket'],'The TGT is used to request service tickets; the service ticket is presented to the target service.','A Kerberos ticket is not a guarantee that the endpoint itself is uncompromised.','Protect service account keys, prefer strong encryption, audit delegation.','Which ticket is normally presented to an application service?',['A service ticket','A subnet mask','An MX record'],0,'The TGS issues a ticket for the specific service.','handshake'),
L('MFA and identity attacks','MFA adds a separate factor, but phishing-resistant MFA provides stronger protection than easily relayed one-time codes.','Two independent locks are useful only if the thief cannot carry both keys away.',['Password or passkey','Additional verification','Bind session to user','Re-check privileged actions'],'FIDO2/WebAuthn passkeys are resistant to lookalike-domain phishing because credentials bind to the origin.','SMS codes and push prompts can be socially engineered.','Use phishing-resistant MFA and monitor anomalous sessions.','Which typically offers phishing resistance?',['FIDO2 security key','Reused password','SMS code alone'],0,'Origin-bound cryptographic authenticators are harder to relay.','auth'),
L('Patch and configuration hygiene','Patch management fixes known defects; secure configuration removes avoidable exposures even when code is current.','A well-built door still needs a lock, and a broken lock needs repair.',['Inventory systems','Prioritize exposures','Test changes','Deploy and verify'],'A fully patched server may still expose an admin console to the Internet.','An up-to-date OS is not automatically hardened.','Reduce services, lock down defaults and validate changes.','Which issue is primarily misconfiguration?',['Public admin interface with default access','CPU instruction decode','Normal scheduled backup'],0,'Improper exposure and default access are insecure settings.'),
L('Backups and recovery','Recovery requires usable, tested backups, defined recovery objectives and a plan for restoring trustworthy systems.','A spare parachute is useful only if it was packed and tested.',['Set RPO/RTO','Keep protected copies','Test restores','Rebuild clean systems'],'RPO is acceptable data loss measured in time; RTO is acceptable time to restore service.','A backup that cannot be restored is not resilience.','Keep isolated or immutable backups and practice recovery.','RTO primarily measures what?',['Maximum acceptable recovery time','Allowed password length','Firewall throughput'],0,'Recovery Time Objective defines the target duration to restore service.','cycle')
]),
W('owasp','The Web Faultline','The OWASP Top 10:2025 explained through attack-and-fix stories','</>','#e99792','query',[
L('A01 — Broken Access Control','A user can perform an action or access an object without the required authorization. IDOR is one common manifestation.','A guest can open a room by changing the room number on a reservation.',['User authenticates','Changes /invoice/41 to /invoice/42','Server omits ownership check','Other customer data exposed'],'GET /api/orders/42 must verify the caller may read order 42, not just that a cookie exists.','Hiding admin buttons in the UI is not a server-side access check.','Enforce deny-by-default checks at object and function level.','What prevents IDOR?',['Object-level authorization','Random-looking IDs alone','Client-side hidden fields'],0,'An authorization check must bind the requested object to the caller.','access','https://owasp.org/Top10/2025/A01_2025-Broken_Access_Control/'),
L('A02 — Security Misconfiguration','Unsafe defaults, unnecessary services, incorrect headers, cloud exposure and verbose error handling can reveal or expose systems.','A safe with a strong lock is useless if the installation manual leaves it open.',['Deploy default config','Expose debug mode','Error reveals internals','Attacker gains clues'],'A production service running with debug=True can leak implementation details in error pages.','Fixing code bugs does not eliminate dangerous deployment settings.','Harden templates, use IaC reviews and continuously verify configuration.','Which is a misconfiguration?',['Public debug console','Prepared SQL statement','Short-lived session'],0,'Debug consoles should not be reachable by untrusted users.','firewall'),
L('A03 — Software Supply Chain Failures','Builds rely on packages, registries, CI workflows and release artifacts; compromise anywhere in the chain can affect delivered software.','A recipe is safe only if its ingredients and delivery chain are trustworthy.',['App imports dependency','Dependency update is compromised','CI builds it','Customers receive malicious code'],'A malicious package typosquats a popular npm library name.','Simply scanning your own source misses dependency and pipeline risks.','Pin and review dependencies, protect CI credentials, generate SBOMs and verify artifacts.','Which practice helps supply-chain integrity?',['Artifact signing and dependency review','Using latest without review','Storing build tokens in code'],0,'Verification and review reduce tampering and dependency risk.','pipeline'),
L('A04 — Cryptographic Failures','Sensitive information can be exposed by missing encryption, poor key handling, obsolete algorithms or weak protocol choices.','A strong safe is ineffective when its key is taped to the door.',['Identify sensitive data','Choose modern crypto','Manage keys safely','Rotate and monitor'],'Storing password hashes with a fast general-purpose hash instead of a dedicated password KDF invites offline guessing.','Encoding (Base64) is not encryption; hashing is not reversible encryption.','Use TLS, authenticated encryption and Argon2id/scrypt/bcrypt for passwords.','Which approach is suitable for password storage?',['Argon2id with unique salts','Plain SHA-256 alone','Base64'],0,'Memory-hard password hashing slows guessing attacks.','hash'),
L('A05 — Injection','Untrusted input becomes commands or query syntax when an application mixes data and instructions.','A customer writes instructions on a form and a clerk mistakenly obeys them.',['Input arrives','String-concatenated query','Input changes query structure','Unexpected result'],'Unsafe: SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = \' + input + \'. Bound parameters instead keep values as data.','Input sanitization is brittle as the only layer; escaping rules vary by context.','Use parameterized queries and avoid shell command construction.','What is the main SQL injection defense?',['Parameterized queries','Changing the page color','Using GET instead of POST'],0,'Bound parameters separate query syntax from data.','query'),
L('A06 — Insecure Design','Design weaknesses arise from missing security requirements and threat models; they cannot always be patched with a filter.','A bank that allows unlimited cash withdrawal without balance checks has a broken rule.',['Model expected behavior','Find abuse cases','Define invariants','Test design controls'],'A transfer endpoint should enforce daily limits and reject negative amounts regardless of input format.','WAF signatures cannot replace correctly designed business rules.','Use threat modeling, abuse-case tests and security acceptance criteria.','A coupon can be redeemed infinitely. What is missing?',['Business-logic constraint','DNS record','TLS certificate'],0,'Usage limits must be part of the workflow design.','cycle'),
L('A07 — Authentication Failures','Weak login, credential recovery, session handling and account lifecycle can permit identity compromise.','A reception desk gives a new key to anyone who knows a guest’s room number.',['Credentials submitted','Weak reset or validation','Session abused','Account taken over'],'A password reset link must be single-use, short-lived and tied to the correct account.','Rate limiting alone does not fix insecure reset logic.','Use MFA, strong recovery flows and secure sessions.','What protects an account reset flow?',['Short-lived one-time token','Predictable token','Username as the only proof'],0,'An unguessable, scoped, short-lived token limits reset abuse.','auth'),
L('A08 — Software or Data Integrity Failures','Applications may trust updates, plugins, serialized data or CI outputs without verifying their integrity and provenance.','Accepting an unsigned delivery into a secure warehouse.',['External artifact received','Trust asserted without proof','Artifact executed or used','Integrity compromised'],'An auto-update mechanism should verify a valid signature before installing an update.','Encrypted transport is useful but not always sufficient to establish artifact provenance.','Sign updates, validate provenance and avoid unsafe deserialization.','What best authenticates a software update?',['Verified digital signature','A .zip file extension','Its small file size'],0,'Digital signatures can verify origin and integrity.','pipeline'),
L('A09 — Logging and Alerting Failures','Insufficient telemetry, unmonitored events and missing response workflows let intrusions persist unnoticed.','A smoke detector with no batteries or nobody listening to its alarm.',['Security event happens','No useful log emitted','Alert never fires','Incident goes undetected'],'Repeated failed logins followed by success should carry user, source, timestamp and outcome.','Collecting thousands of logs without response plans is not effective detection.','Log security events, alert on meaningful patterns and test response.','Which is most useful in an auth event?',['User, time, source, result','CSS theme','Browser tab color'],0,'These fields support correlation and investigation.','triage'),
L('A10 — Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions','Unexpected states, errors, edge cases and failures can cause security controls to fail open or reveal sensitive details.','A broken door reader defaults to unlocked instead of safely closed.',['Unexpected input or outage','Unhandled exception','Security check bypasses','Access or data exposed'],'If an authorization service times out, the caller should not receive access by default.','Catching every error and continuing may silently bypass a critical security step.','Fail securely, handle errors explicitly and test boundary cases.','An authorization dependency times out. Safer default?',['Deny and handle the error','Allow everyone','Disable audit logging'],0,'Fail-closed behavior avoids giving access when authorization cannot be verified.','access')
]),
W('offensive','Attack & Validate','Learn how authorized security assessments actually work','⌁','#b6bd88','attack',[
L('Rules of engagement','A legitimate assessment starts with written scope, allowed methods, time windows and reporting agreements.','A building inspection needs a signed work order defining which doors can be tested.',['Agree on scope','Choose safe methods','Collect minimum evidence','Deliver remediation'],'A lab scope may allow scanning 10.10.10.0/24 but prohibit production systems.','Discovering a vulnerability does not grant permission to exploit it further.','Use explicit authorization and stop at agreed proof.','What comes before active testing?',['Documented permission and scope','Public target list','Publishing screenshots'],0,'Authorization defines what testing is legitimate.'),
L('Recon vs enumeration','Reconnaissance gathers information; scanning probes reachable services; enumeration asks detailed questions about discovered services.','Look up a building, check which doors exist, then read door labels.',['Passive recon','Discovery scanning','Service identification','Scoped enumeration'],'In an authorized lab, finding TCP 443 open suggests checking the web service and its version.','An open port is evidence of a service, not automatically a vulnerability.','Correlate versions, exposure and configuration before making a finding.','An open TCP port alone proves compromise.',['True','False','Only on Linux'],1,'An open port only indicates a service accepting connections.','attack'),
L('Vulnerability validation','Validate the preconditions and actual impact of suspected vulnerabilities without causing unnecessary harm.','A building inspector tests one door rather than damaging every entrance.',['Form a hypothesis','Check configuration','Reproduce safely','Document proof and fix'],'A scanner flags missing security headers; verify the actual response and relevant threat.','A scanner finding is a lead, not a verified exploit.','Prefer non-destructive evidence and verify remediation.','What belongs in a useful finding?',['Evidence, impact, fix and scope','Only a scary CVSS number','An untested guess'],0,'Actionable findings require context and reproduction details.'),
L('Cross-site scripting','XSS happens when untrusted content is interpreted as active browser code in an unsafe output context.','A note meant as text gets read aloud as an instruction by the speaker.',['Untrusted value','Inserted into DOM','Browser interprets code','Script executes in origin'],'Safe example: render the string <script>alert(1)</script> as plain text with textContent.','Escaping for HTML text is not automatically safe inside JavaScript, URL or attributes.','Use context-aware output encoding, safe DOM APIs and CSP as defense in depth.','Which API renders user text without parsing HTML?',['textContent','innerHTML','document.write'],0,'textContent inserts text without treating it as markup.','query'),
L('Server-side request forgery','SSRF occurs when a server fetches a user-controlled URL and can reach destinations the user cannot.','A visitor persuades a trusted messenger to enter a staff-only room.',['User submits URL','Server makes request','Internal address reachable','Sensitive response or side effect'],'A preview service should not be able to fetch loopback, metadata endpoints or private addresses on user demand.','A hostname allowlist can be bypassed by unsafe redirects or DNS behavior if not handled.','Use strict destination policies, revalidate redirects and restrict egress.','Which control most directly limits SSRF blast radius?',['Network egress restrictions','Bigger HTML font','A public admin password'],0,'Egress controls stop the server from reaching sensitive networks.','cloud'),
L('Path traversal and file access','Traversal abuses unsafe file-path construction to reach files outside the permitted directory.','A librarian accepts ../ in a book request and walks into the archive.',['Input becomes path','Path resolves outside base','Sensitive file requested','Application returns it'],'A download handler should map opaque file IDs to approved storage objects, not trust raw filenames.','Removing the literal ../ string is insufficient if normalization and encoding are mishandled.','Resolve canonical paths and enforce approved base directories.','Which design avoids client-chosen file paths?',['Server-side ID to approved path mapping','Concatenating user input','Allowing absolute paths'],0,'Opaque IDs keep arbitrary filesystem paths out of request data.','access'),
L('Threat modeling and STRIDE','STRIDE helps examine spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege across flows.','Six different questions to ask about every checkpoint in a building.',['Draw components','Mark trust boundaries','Apply threat categories','Choose mitigations'],'At a login API: spoofing might mean stolen identity; tampering might mean changed parameters.','STRIDE is a brainstorming method, not a vulnerability scanner.','Model data flows, assets and assumptions before selecting controls.','Which STRIDE category covers pretending to be another user?',['Spoofing','Repudiation','Denial of service'],0,'Spoofing concerns identity impersonation.','layers'),
L('Responsible findings','An excellent report explains reproducibility, affected asset, business impact and pragmatic remediation.','The repair crew needs exact location and instructions, not merely “something is broken.”',['Title and scope','Evidence and reproduction','Impact and severity','Remediation and retest'],'Finding: endpoint lacks object authorization; include redacted request pairs and recommended ownership checks.','Avoid including real tokens or personal data in public reports.','Redact secrets and keep raw evidence in authorized secure channels.','What should public report screenshots exclude?',['Credentials and private data','The finding title','The affected feature'],0,'Sensitive data should be redacted before sharing.','pipeline')
]),
W('defense','Blue Team Operations','From telemetry to decisions under pressure','◉','#9eb6dc','triage',[
L('What a SOC does','A SOC monitors, triages and responds to security activity using telemetry, threat intelligence and playbooks.','An air-traffic control room for security events, not just a wall of alerts.',['Collect signals','Detect suspicious activity','Triage context','Contain and learn'],'A detection fires for a script interpreter spawning from an Office document; the analyst investigates parent process and user context.','Not every alert is an incident, and not every incident triggers an alert.','Build hypotheses, validate evidence and improve detections.','An alert fires. What comes before containment?',['Triage and scope','Delete all logs','Restart every system'],0,'Determine legitimacy, risk and scope to choose an appropriate response.','triage'),
L('SIEM and correlation','A SIEM stores and searches event data, normalizes fields and correlates patterns across sources.','Security events are puzzle pieces; correlation lets you see the whole picture.',['Collect logs','Normalize fields','Join by entity and time','Alert on patterns'],'Many failed logins followed by success from the same source can warrant investigation.','A SIEM is only as effective as telemetry coverage and detection quality.','Log process, identity, network and cloud actions with synchronized time.','What makes two logs correlate usefully?',['Shared host/user/time context','Matching background color','Their file size'],0,'Shared entities and temporal relationships connect events.','triage'),
L('Incident response lifecycle','Preparation, detection/analysis, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned form a practical incident cycle.','A fire crew detects smoke, isolates the hazard, removes it, rebuilds and reviews.',['Prepare and detect','Analyze and contain','Eradicate and recover','Improve playbooks'],'An infected endpoint may be isolated after triage while responders preserve volatile evidence.','Wiping first can destroy evidence and leave the root cause unknown.','Maintain playbooks, backups, contacts and forensic preservation procedures.','Why capture evidence before destructive cleanup when feasible?',['To preserve the timeline and root cause','To make alerts louder','To improve Wi-Fi'],0,'Evidence is often lost during reimaging and cleanup.','cycle'),
L('Log fields and timelines','Timestamps, hostnames, user IDs, IPs, process trees and outcomes help turn isolated records into a sequence.','A detective needs who, where, when and what happened.',['Normalize time zones','Group entities','Build event order','Explain uncertainty'],'Parent winword.exe spawning powershell.exe may be meaningful in the context of a suspicious attachment.','Process name alone is rarely conclusive without command line and context.','Collect process creation and authentication telemetry.','Which evidence adds context to a process alert?',['Parent process and command line','Wallpaper color','Screen resolution'],0,'Lineage and arguments clarify whether execution looks expected.','triage'),
L('MITRE ATT&CK','Tactics describe adversary goals; techniques describe how those goals are achieved. ATT&CK helps map observable behavior and coverage gaps.','A playbook records both an opponent’s objective and moves.',['Observe behavior','Map likely technique','Find supporting telemetry','Design detection'],'Credential Access is a tactic; OS Credential Dumping is one example technique.','ATT&CK is not a strict chronological kill chain or proof of attribution.','Map behaviors, not just tool names, and keep detection coverage current.','In ATT&CK, a tactic describes what?',['The adversary’s goal','A Windows patch','A random IP address'],0,'Tactics capture why an adversary acts.','matrix','https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/enterprise/'),
L('Threat hunting','Threat hunting proactively tests evidence-based hypotheses when automated detections may miss behavior.','A detective looks for patterns a door alarm was never designed to catch.',['Write hypothesis','Identify data sources','Query and pivot','Record conclusion'],'Hypothesis: an account used from two distant locations within an implausible time frame.','An unusual event is not by itself malicious; consider travel and VPN usage.','Document assumptions and convert validated patterns into repeatable detections.','What comes first in a hypothesis-led hunt?',['Testable behavior hypothesis','Random log deletion','Automatic account lockout'],0,'The hunt begins with a testable proposition.','triage'),
L('Email and phishing triage','Phishing analysis compares the claimed sender with authentication results, links, attachments and the intended action.','An official-looking envelope may contain forged return details.',['Examine headers','Check domain and links','Inspect behavior safely','Scope other recipients'],'SPF, DKIM and DMARC contribute to email authentication, but a legitimate compromised mailbox can still phish.','Good SPF alignment does not guarantee benign content.','Use attachment isolation, domain reputation and reporting workflows.','A valid DKIM signature proves an email is harmless.',['True','False','Only with HTML'],1,'It helps authenticate signing integrity, not the intent of the message.','pipeline'),
L('Detection engineering','A high-quality detection connects a behavior to telemetry, defines expected baselines and measures false positives.','A good smoke alarm triggers for smoke, not every time someone makes toast.',['Define threat behavior','Choose data fields','Write detection logic','Test and tune'],'Alert on suspicious administrative group changes by unusual actors, enriched with privileged access history.','An alert with no suppression, context or runbook can overwhelm responders.','Add tests, severity logic, field requirements and owner.','Which measure describes benign events incorrectly flagged?',['False positives','True positives','Packet loss'],0,'False positives are normal activity flagged as suspicious.','triage'),
L('Ransomware response','Ransomware incidents often involve initial compromise, lateral movement, data theft and encryption or extortion.','A thief steals documents before locking all cabinets.',['Identify patient zero','Isolate active spread','Protect clean backups','Recover after eradication'],'Contain infected hosts and compromised credentials, and evaluate potential exfiltration before restoring.','A successful file restore does not mean stolen data was never copied.','Segment networks, minimize privilege and test offline recovery.','Why investigate exfiltration after restoring backups?',['Recovery does not reverse data theft','It always fixes DNS','It changes the RPO'],0,'Data confidentiality risk remains even after availability is restored.','cycle')
]),
W('cryptography','Keys & Proofs','Understand what crypto actually guarantees','◇','#d5c486','handshake',[
L('Encoding, hashing, encryption','Encoding changes representation, hashing produces a one-way digest, and encryption uses keys to recover protected plaintext.','Writing in another alphabet vs a fingerprint vs a locked box.',['Encoding: reversible format','Hashing: digest','Encryption: keyed transform','Choose for the goal'],'Base64 is encoding; SHA-256 is a hash; AES-GCM is authenticated encryption.','Do not store passwords using reversible encryption or plain fast hashes.','Use password KDFs for credentials, modern AEAD for confidential data.','Which operation is designed to be reversed with a secret key?',['Encryption','Hashing','Checksum'],0,'Encryption is reversible for authorized parties holding the key.','hash'),
L('Symmetric encryption and AES','Symmetric ciphers use the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt; authenticated modes also detect modifications.','Both people share a locked box key.',['Create random key','Choose unique nonce','Encrypt + authenticate','Verify tag on decrypt'],'AES-GCM protects confidentiality and integrity when its nonce requirements are met.','Reusing a GCM nonce with the same key can catastrophically break security.','Use well-tested cryptographic libraries and safe nonce generation.','What is unsafe with AES-GCM?',['Nonce reuse under one key','Random unique nonces','Verifying the tag'],0,'GCM critically depends on nonce uniqueness.','hash'),
L('Public-key cryptography','Public/private key pairs enable signatures and key agreement without distributing one shared secret to everyone.','A public padlock anyone can close, paired with a private key only one person holds.',['Generate key pair','Distribute public key','Prove possession privately','Validate identity binding'],'TLS certificates help bind a hostname to a public key through trusted validation.','A public key alone does not tell you who owns it.','Validate certificates, signatures and identity bindings.','Which key should remain secret?',['Private key','Public key','Certificate subject'],0,'The private key is the secret half of the pair.','handshake'),
L('Hash integrity and signatures','A hash detects changes only if the trusted expected digest is authentic; a digital signature binds a signed message to a key.','A fingerprint comparison works if the original fingerprint record is reliable.',['Hash content','Sign digest/context','Distribute artifact','Verify signature'],'Package signing helps verify releases even when downloaded from a mirror.','Publishing hash and file on the same compromised page gives weak authenticity assurance.','Use trusted signing keys and verified release channels.','What distinguishes a signature from a bare hash?',['Key-based authenticity','Smaller file size','Guaranteed secrecy'],0,'Signatures let a verifier authenticate origin/integrity with a trusted public key.','hash'),
L('Password salts and KDFs','A unique random salt ensures identical passwords do not share the same stored hash; a slow, memory-hard KDF increases guessing cost.','Every lock receives a different custom fitting; copying a master list is less useful.',['Generate unique salt','Apply Argon2id / scrypt','Store salt + output','Verify by recomputing'],'Salts are stored alongside password hashes and need not be secret.','A salt is not a replacement for strong user passwords or MFA.','Tune KDF cost to your environment and apply credential defenses.','Should a password salt be secret?',['No, but it should be unique and random','Yes, always','It must match every user'],0,'Salts are not keys; uniqueness blocks precomputed reuse.','hash'),
L('PKI and certificate trust','PKI connects digital certificates, issuing authorities, public keys and validation rules.','A trusted licensing office signs identity documents that recipients verify.',['Server sends certificate','Check chain and hostname','Validate dates and policy','Establish secure channel'],'A certificate for example.com should not authenticate a different unrelated hostname.','Accepting all certificates disables a critical identity check.','Enable proper hostname and certificate validation.','Why is hostname validation important?',['It binds cert to requested service','It makes packets faster','It enables VLAN tags'],0,'Without name checks, a valid certificate for another site may be misused.','handshake')
]),
W('cloud','Cloud & Containers','Defend infrastructure beyond a single host','☁','#8abfbb','cloud',[
L('Shared responsibility','The cloud provider protects parts of the underlying service; customers still own identities, data, configuration and many workloads.','A landlord maintains the building, tenants lock their own doors.',['Provider secures facilities','Customer manages access','Roles vary by service','Validate configurations'],'Managed storage does not make public bucket permissions safe by default.','Shared responsibility does not mean equal responsibility for every service model.','Review specific service responsibilities and defaults.','Who configures access to your cloud data?',['Usually the customer','Always the ISP','Only the certificate authority'],0,'Customers remain responsible for their data access policies.','cloud'),
L('IAM and least privilege','Cloud IAM policies decide which principal may take an action against a resource under conditions.','An office badge grants one person access to certain rooms during certain hours.',['Principal','Action','Resource','Conditions / explicit denies'],'Allow a reporting role only object:Get against the approved report bucket.','Broad wildcard permissions create unnecessary compromise impact.','Use roles, temporary credentials and permission boundaries.','Which IAM grant is safer?',['Read-only on one bucket','Full admin on all accounts','Public write to every bucket'],0,'Narrow resource and action permissions reduce blast radius.','access'),
L('Networking in cloud','VPCs, security groups, ACLs, routes and private endpoints determine which cloud resources can communicate.','Virtual streets, gates and guarded service entrances.',['Segment subnets','Define routes','Allow needed flows','Inspect logs'],'Expose a load balancer publicly while keeping database nodes on private subnets.','A private subnet is not automatically safe from compromised workloads.','Restrict east-west movement and private service access.','Where should a production DB usually live?',['Restricted private network','Open public subnet','Unfiltered test LAN'],0,'Restricting inbound reachability reduces exposure.','cloud'),
L('Containers and images','Containers share the host kernel but isolate processes, filesystems and network namespaces; images are layered templates.','Multiple cabins share one ship engine while using separate doors and supplies.',['Build image layers','Start isolated process','Apply resource limits','Monitor host and runtime'],'A container run as root may increase impact, especially with privileged mounts or host access.','A container is not the same security boundary as a full independent VM.','Use minimal images, non-root users, read-only FS and restricted capabilities.','Do containers normally share a host kernel?',['Yes','No, each boots its own kernel','Only on IPv6'],0,'Containers rely on the same kernel with isolation features.','container'),
L('Kubernetes essentials','Kubernetes orchestrates pods, deployments, services and policy. RBAC and network policy limit cluster access and pod communication.','A harbor scheduler assigns containers to docks and controls access routes.',['Deployment declares desired state','Pods run containers','Services expose endpoints','Policies restrict access'],'A NetworkPolicy can restrict which pods communicate with a database pod if supported by the CNI.','A Kubernetes Service is not automatically an Internet-facing service.','Enable RBAC, admission controls, resource limits and pod security settings.','What expresses desired replica count?',['Deployment','TXT DNS record','Kernel panic'],0,'A Deployment controller maintains the specified replica count.','container'),
L('CI/CD and secrets','Delivery pipelines build and ship code; workflow permissions and secret handling are security-critical trust boundaries.','An automated factory line must verify workers, supplies and dispatch stamps.',['Source trigger','Build and test','Sign / attest output','Deploy least privilege'],'An untrusted pull request must not gain production deployment credentials.','Putting secrets into repo history leaves them accessible even after editing the latest file.','Use short-lived identity federation, secret stores and branch protection.','Which is safer for CI cloud access?',['Short-lived scoped credentials','Permanent admin key in repo','Shared password in script'],0,'Ephemeral scoped credentials limit credential theft impact.','pipeline'),
L('Observability and posture','Cloud audit logs, flow logs and configuration monitoring answer who changed what, where and when.','Cameras and inventory sensors across virtual buildings.',['Enable audit trail','Collect and retain logs','Detect drift','Remediate exposure'],'Unexpected changes to object-storage public-access settings should trigger investigation.','A dashboard alone is not a response capability.','Alert on risky config changes and practice rollback.','Which log records cloud control-plane API actions?',['Cloud audit/activity trail','Monitor brightness','Desktop wallpaper history'],0,'Cloud audit services track API actions and actors.','triage'),
L('Zero trust architecture','Zero trust continuously evaluates explicit identity, device and context instead of relying on network location as proof of trust.','A secure lab validates each lab-room access, even after someone enters the building.',['Verify explicitly','Least-privilege authorization','Assume compromise','Reassess context'],'A VPN connection should not automatically grant access to every internal service.','Zero trust is not one vendor product or a promise of absolute security.','Use strong identity, device posture, segmentation and monitoring.','In zero trust, internal traffic is…',['Still subject to verification','Automatically trusted','Never allowed'],0,'Location by itself is not sufficient proof of legitimacy.','access')
]),
W('advanced','Adversaries & Strategy','Join tactical behavior, engineering and risk','✦','#e0a7bb','matrix',[
L('ATT&CK tactic vs technique','A tactic is the adversary objective, a technique is a method, and a procedure is a concrete observed implementation.','Goal: enter a building. Method: borrowed badge. Procedure: exact entrance used.',['Tactic: goal','Technique: how','Procedure: exact behavior','Map to detection'],'Initial Access is a tactic; phishing is a technique family; one specific campaign email is a procedure.','ATT&CK mapping is descriptive; an observed technique alone does not identify an actor.','Track behavior, observables and detection confidence.','Which is a procedure?',['A specific command sequence used in a case','The broad goal of persistence','All cybercrime'],0,'A procedure is the concrete way a technique is implemented.','matrix','https://attack.mitre.org/'),
L('Privilege paths and graphs','Graph thinking reveals how seemingly limited accounts can gain powerful access through group nesting, sessions or delegated permissions.','Several ordinary doors form an unexpected corridor to a vault.',['Nodes: users and hosts','Edges: rights and sessions','Find risky paths','Remove unnecessary edges'],'A low-trust account allowed to reset a privileged service account password creates a dangerous relationship.','A graph edge suggests a possible path, not proof it is currently exploitable.','Reduce excessive delegations and audit privileged edges.','What does an edge in an access graph represent?',['A relationship or permission','An encryption key always','A CPU core'],0,'Edges encode permission or session relationships.','access'),
L('Detection as code','Store detection rules with version history, field contracts, tests and deployment review like application code.','Unit tests and code review for alarms before they go live.',['Write behavior rule','Create positive/negative fixtures','Test fields and logic','Review and deploy'],'A rule for high-risk process lineage should include one malicious test event and one benign counterexample.','A rule can have syntactically valid code but still have unusably high false positives.','Measure telemetry coverage and tune after release.','What should negative tests verify?',['Benign activity does not trigger unnecessarily','That the rule file is pretty','That a server has a logo'],0,'Negative tests guard against noisy false positives.','triage'),
L('Malware analysis levels','Static inspection examines files without executing them; dynamic analysis observes execution in an isolated instrumented environment.','Read a suspicious device blueprint, then test it in a sealed workshop.',['Collect safely','Inspect metadata and strings','Execute only in isolation','Capture behavior and indicators'],'A sandbox may observe registry persistence or network callbacks; results depend on evasion and environment.','Running untrusted binaries on your primary machine is dangerous.','Use isolated environments and safe handling procedures.','Which analysis examines a file without execution?',['Static','Dynamic','Packet routing'],0,'Static analysis inspects bytes, structure and metadata without running the sample.','container'),
L('Risk and security decisions','Security decisions compare the likelihood and consequence of risk with cost, business needs and acceptable residual exposure.','An insurer prioritizes high-impact threats while still running the business.',['Identify scenario','Estimate likelihood/impact','Select treatment','Track residual risk'],'A low-cost exposed development key may demand quick rotation despite low asset value because it could access production.','CVSS base severity does not fully describe your business-specific risk.','Maintain an owner, deadline, treatment and evidence.','Which item describes residual risk?',['Risk remaining after controls','Risk before any control','Guaranteed zero incidents'],0,'Residual risk is what remains after treatment.','cycle'),
L('NIST CSF 2.0','The framework groups cybersecurity outcomes into Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover. It guides risk management, not specific tool purchases.','A complete emergency plan covers leadership through restoring service.',['Govern & Identify','Protect','Detect & Respond','Recover'],'Govern establishes risk expectations and ownership; Recover restores services and communications after incidents.','The older five-function list omits Govern from CSF 2.0.','Map organizational practices and gaps to the six functions.','Which function was added in CSF 2.0?',['Govern','Exploit','Encrypt'],0,'Govern became an explicit core function in CSF 2.0.','cycle','https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework'),
L('Threat-informed architecture','Use credible adversary behaviors to decide which identity, network, data and detection controls matter most.','Design the fortress against realistic entry paths, not imaginary monsters.',['Choose critical assets','Model attacker goals','Map likely paths','Layer controls and evidence'],'For a customer portal, protect auth, session, API ownership checks, rate limits, logs and recovery together.','Buying more tools is not equivalent to reducing risk.','Validate assumptions in tabletop exercises and attack simulations.','What best grounds an architectural control?',['Threat scenario and asset impact','Vendor popularity','Random scan output'],0,'A control is stronger when tied to explicit threats and outcomes.','layers'),
L('Incident tabletop exercise','A tabletop tests decisions and communication with a realistic scenario without disrupting production systems.','A flight crew rehearse emergencies in a simulator.',['Present initial evidence','Choose roles and decisions','Inject new facts','Capture improvement actions'],'A ransomware tabletop asks when to isolate, who owns legal escalation and how service restoration is validated.','Tabletops do not prove tools work technically; pair them with drills.','Assign owners to lessons and rehearse again after fixes.','What is a primary tabletop deliverable?',['Decision gaps and action owners','A stolen password','Production outage'],0,'Tabletops expose process and coordination improvements.','cycle')
])
];
window.ALL_LESSONS=window.CURRICULUM.flatMap(w=>w.lessons);
